WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.300
An adult human holds about 5 litres of blood.

2
00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:12.760
At rest, each side of the heart pumps roughly that much every minute.

3
00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:16.940
So the same few litres go round again and again.

4
00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:19.380
How does a pump manage that?

5
00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:22.960
Fluid flows from higher pressure to lower.

6
00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:26.120
So a pump works by making a pressure difference.

7
00:00:26.950 --> 00:00:29.290
Take a mammal after birth.

8
00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:34.370
Oxygen-poor blood from the body enters the right atrium.

9
00:00:35.390 --> 00:00:37.770
The right ventricle pushes it to the lungs.

10
00:00:39.290 --> 00:00:42.450
Oxygen-rich blood returns to the left atrium.

11
00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:46.150
The left ventricle pushes it on to the body.

12
00:00:47.510 --> 00:00:50.790
Right and left mean the animal's own right and left.

13
00:00:51.683 --> 00:00:56.023
Now follow the left ventricle of an adult human at rest.

14
00:00:56.783 --> 00:01:00.643
Each valve opens when pressure behind it exceeds pressure ahead.

15
00:01:01.503 --> 00:01:02.683
First comes filling.

16
00:01:03.603 --> 00:01:08.643
Ventricular pressure is below atrial pressure, so the mitral valve is open and blood

17
00:01:08.643 --> 00:01:13.043
flows in. The atrium's own contraction adds the last part.

18
00:01:13.967 --> 00:01:16.287
Then the ventricle contracts.

19
00:01:17.087 --> 00:01:21.747
Its pressure rises above atrial pressure, and the mitral valve closes.

20
00:01:22.447 --> 00:01:26.907
But it is still below aortic pressure, so the aortic valve stays shut.

21
00:01:27.847 --> 00:01:31.287
Both valves are closed, so the volume cannot change.

22
00:01:32.067 --> 00:01:33.767
Blood cannot enter or leave.

23
00:01:34.587 --> 00:01:37.547
This phase is called isovolumic contraction.

24
00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:41.170
Ventricular pressure keeps rising.

25
00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:47.570
When it passes aortic pressure, about 80 mmHg, the aortic valve

26
00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:49.510
opens and blood leaves.

27
00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:54.470
Both pressures peak near 120 mmHg.

28
00:01:55.290 --> 00:02:00.930
Then the ventricle relaxes, its pressure falls below the aorta's, and the aortic

29
00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:06.450
valve closes. Both valves are shut until the mitral valve opens again.

30
00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:13.670
So in the reading 120 over 80, the 80 is the aorta's lowest pressure.

31
00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:15.290
It is not the ventricle's.

32
00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:19.690
The ventricle falls to a few mmHg as it fills.

33
00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:25.170
The right ventricle runs the same cycle against the low resistance of the lungs.

34
00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:28.550
It peaks near 25 mmHg.

35
00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:32.250
It ejects the same volume at a fifth of the pressure.

36
00:02:33.070 --> 00:02:38.630
Keep this rule. A valve opens when pressure behind it exceeds pressure ahead.
